WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:04.520
It sounds broken...

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:10.040
In 2018, the average American
will spend $600 on holiday gifts,

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.340
and total spending in the US will
top $700 billion. And more than

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:19.400
$100 billion of that spending will be
totally wasted, or as economists like to say,

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.100
those gifts will be a deadweight loss.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.640
How can you avoid such a loss this year?

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.840
Economic theory provides a simple solution: Give cash!

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:34.220
Yale economist Joel Waldfogel — conducting
serious science — wrote a famous article that

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:39.540
estimated that between one-tenth and
one-third of holiday gift-giving is mismatched

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:45.180
compared to a cash gift. Waldfogel finds further
support from economist Steven Landsburg,

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:50.640
who says he's not sure why people give each other
store-bought gifts like clothing instead of cash,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.360
because cash is never the wrong
size or color. So, clearly cash is always

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.080
the most efficient gift. But if
cash seems too impersonal,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:03.040
consider the next most
economically efficient gift — a gift card.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.660
Like cash, it's never the wrong size or color.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.340
Do you think cash is an acceptable
holiday gift? Let us know in our poll.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:16.960
Also, let us know what other topic you'd
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.500
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe
for more research and videos from AEI.

